WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.820
A neuron has no label saying where it came from.

2
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.100
So how do you find out?

3
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.700
A birth date marks one cohort of cells.

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.560
A lineage is one family.

5
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.100
To follow one, write a mark into a cell's own DNA.

6
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.420
Then every descendant carries it.

7
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.120
The tool that does this is an enzyme called Cre recombinase.

8
00:00:21.933 --> 00:00:24.253
Start with the gene for a fluorescent protein.

9
00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:27.213
A reporter like this shows where a cell is.

10
00:00:27.713 --> 00:00:30.693
In front of it, place a transcriptional stop signal.

11
00:00:31.113 --> 00:00:33.793
Put a LOX -P site on each side of that stop.

12
00:00:34.393 --> 00:00:38.473
Each site is a short DNA sequence, 34 base pairs long.

13
00:00:38.993 --> 00:00:40.653
This is a cre -dependent reporter.

14
00:00:41.313 --> 00:00:43.953
While the stop is in place, the gene is not read.

15
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:47.437
Now Kree recombinase appears in a cell.

16
00:00:47.977 --> 00:00:50.097
It deletes the stop that the sites flank.

17
00:00:50.757 --> 00:00:53.197
The reporter is read, and the cell glows.

18
00:00:53.937 --> 00:00:56.797
Kree is made from a promoter active in one cell type.

19
00:00:57.397 --> 00:00:59.777
Only cells that make Kree delete the stop.

20
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:01.437
Their neighbors stay dark.

21
00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:03.317
Now that cell divides.

22
00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:07.977
The deletion is in its DNA, so both daughters inherit it.

23
00:01:08.397 --> 00:01:12.257
The reporter stays read in its descendants while its promoter is active.

24
00:01:12.877 --> 00:01:13.917
The marking is permanent.

25
00:01:14.577 --> 00:01:18.417
A cell -type driver marks many founders, so this gives a fate map.

26
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.660
Kree can also remove a gene.

27
00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.960
Flank an important part of a gene with LOX -P sites.

28
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.300
That allele is called floxed, and it is designed to work normally.

29
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.560
Supply Kree from a promoter active in one cell type, and the gene is deleted mostly

30
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.460
there. A whole -body knockout cannot tell you which tissue mattered.

31
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.380
For timing, fuse Kree to a modified estrogen receptor.

32
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.360
Then the deletion waits for the drug tamoxifen.

33
00:01:45.083 --> 00:01:48.823
One trap. A reporter cross tells you where Kree acted.

34
00:01:49.243 --> 00:01:51.923
It does not tell you where the floxed gene was lost.

35
00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:55.263
The two lox -flanked targets recombine independently.

36
00:01:55.963 --> 00:01:57.683
Drivers can be leaky or mosaic.

37
00:01:58.343 --> 00:02:00.283
Protein made before the cut persists.

38
00:02:00.863 --> 00:02:02.363
So measure the loss itself.

39
00:02:02.923 --> 00:02:06.363
A reporter shows where Kree acted, not where the gene is gone.
